  "tool_comparison": {
    "kubectl_top": "equals memory.current (all page cache included, inactive_file too): e.g. 1,326,156 Ki = 1,295 MiB at startup, 2,486,916 Ki = 2,429 MiB after 1 request, matching memory.current, not memory.current - inactive_file (874 / 2,008 MiB).",
    "docker_stats": "equals memory.current - inactive_file: 1.06 GiB = 1,085 MiB, 1.77 GiB = 1,813 MiB, 1.558 GiB = 1,595 MiB, 1.796 GiB = 1,839 MiB.",
    "conclusion": "On this OrbStack setup the two tools count page cache differently: kubectl top includes inactive file cache, docker stats subtracts it. So kubectl top over-states memory that is actually committed by 250-430 MiB here, and round-1 'peak working set' numbers include cache."
  },
  "growth": {
    "anon_after_requests_k8s_mib": {
      "1": 1118,
      "5": 1120,
      "20": 1123
    },
    "anon_after_requests_docker_mib": {
      "1": 1121,
      "5": 1126,
      "20": 1131
    },
    "conclusion": "No leak: anonymous memory rises about 335 MiB on the first request (one-time setup and allocator caching, retained) and then stays flat (+5 to +10 MiB over 19 more requests). File-backed memory grows with page cache until the limit forces reclaim (k8s) or keeps growing (docker, no limit)."
  },
  "summary": {
    "where_memory_goes": {
      "model_weights_and_runtime": "about 785 MiB anonymous at startup plus about 450-600 MiB of the model files mapped into the process (RssFile); the weight files (~1.2 GiB on disk) also sit in page cache",
      "first_request_one_time": "about +335 MiB anonymous retained after the first request (allocator caching / lazy init), then flat",
      "activations_per_rerank": "one rerank is a single 20 x 512-token forward pass; anonymous memory peaks at about 1.20-1.22 GiB during it, i.e. roughly 0.24-0.42 GiB above the level just before the request (k8s request 20: 967 -> 1,203 MiB; request 1: 785 -> 1,209 MiB). Same in Docker with 10 threads.",
      "page_cache": "file-backed memory rises to 1.0-1.3 GiB after the first request and fills the cgroup up to its limit: memory.peak reached 2,559 MiB (the 2,560 MiB limit) in k8s with only ONE request at a time, without any OOM, because inactive cache is reclaimed",
      "growth": "none beyond the first request"
    },
    "round1_explained": "Committed memory for one in-flight rerank is about 1.2 GiB anonymous plus the mapped model pages (~0.5-0.6 GiB). Each additional concurrent rerank adds roughly another 0.25-0.4 GiB of activations (an estimate from these single-request peaks, not measured under concurrency). Three concurrent reranks therefore approach the 2.5 GiB limit (round 1: slow, 2,467 MiB, heavy reclaim of mapped model pages likely), and six exceed it (round 1: OOMKilled).",
    "fix_supported_by_numbers": {
      "limit_to_one_rerank_at_a_time_per_pod": "best supported: per-pod committed memory stays at about 1.2 GiB anon + mapped weights (~1.8 GiB), well under 2.5 GiB at any load; throughput should not drop because reranking is CPU-bound on 2 CPUs (round 1: 3 concurrent reranks on 1 pod gave 0.060 req/s vs 0.091 for 1).",
      "smaller_rerank_batch": "partly supported: activation memory scales with batch size, so batches of 4-5 would cut the ~0.25-0.4 GiB per-rerank peak several-fold, but memory would still grow with concurrency; a useful complement, not a fix on its own.",
      "higher_memory_limit": "least supported on its own: page cache fills any limit anyway, and covering 6 concurrent reranks would need roughly 3.5-4 GiB per pod, which 3 replicas on this 7.8 GiB node cannot fit."
    },
    "caveats": [
      "single pod, sequential requests; concurrency effects are extrapolated from single-request peaks",
      "0.1 s sampling can miss the very top of short spikes",
      "the Docker container had no limits and 10 torch threads vs 2 in k8s; anonymous peaks were nevertheless similar"
    ]
  },
